### Lab Exercise: Telnet vs. SSH TCP Stream Analysis

1\. What information was visible when you followed the Telnet TCP stream?\
I could see the transmitted data, including usernames, passwords, and commands, in readable plaintext.

2\. What changed when you followed the SSH TCP stream?\
The SSH stream displayed encrypted data that was unreadable, making it difficult to identify the transmitted credentials and commands.

3\. Why is sending authentication information over an unencrypted session dangerous?\
Attackers who intercept the traffic can read usernames and passwords and use them to gain unauthorized access to systems.

4\. What security property does SSH provide that Telnet does not?\
SSH provides encryption, protecting transmitted data from being read by unauthorized parties. It also supports authentication and data integrity.

5\. Why should packet captures containing credentials never be uploaded to a public GitHub repository?\
Public packet captures may expose passwords, usernames, and other sensitive information, allowing attackers to misuse credentials or compromise systems.
